      <h2>1. What Was Omegle?</h2>

      <p>Omegle was an online communication service that randomly paired users for conversations with strangers. One of its defining characteristics was that users could begin chatting without building a conventional social-media profile.</p>

      <h3>1.1 The Original Idea</h3>

      <p>The concept was deliberately simple: connect two strangers and let them decide what to talk about.</p>

      <p>That simplicity was part of Omegle's appeal. Users could encounter people outside their normal social circles, including people from different countries and backgrounds.</p>

      <h3>1.2 Text and Video Communication</h3>

      <p>Omegle became associated with both text-based and video-based random conversations. The video format made interactions more immediate, but it also introduced additional moderation and safety challenges because users could communicate through live audio and video.</p>

      <h3>1.3 Why Anonymity Was Both an Advantage and a Problem</h3>

      <p>Not requiring a traditional identity or social profile lowered the barrier to starting a conversation. At the same time, anonymity can make harmful behavior harder to attribute to a real-world identity.</p>

      <p>This tension between spontaneous communication and user safety became one of the central challenges of random-chat services.</p>

      <h2>Why Did Omegle's Anonymity Matter?</h2>

