      <h2>3. Legal Pressure, Safety Concerns and the Changing Internet</h2>

      <p>Omegle's shutdown occurred during a period when governments, regulators and technology companies were paying increasing attention to online safety, particularly the protection of children and the handling of harmful user-generated content.</p>

      <h3>3.1 Online Safety Became a Bigger Policy Issue</h3>

      <p>Regulators in several countries introduced or proposed stronger online safety requirements during the years surrounding Omegle's closure. These developments increased the importance of moderation, reporting, risk assessment and user protection for online services.</p>

      <p>However, it would be misleading to say that one particular law directly caused Omegle to shut down. The shutdown should instead be understood as the result of multiple pressures that accumulated over time.</p>

      <h3>3.2 The Economics of Running a Large Random-Chat Service</h3>

      <p>Safety systems require infrastructure, moderation processes, engineering work, legal resources and ongoing maintenance. Those costs can become particularly difficult for services that do not have a conventional subscription or transaction-based business model.</p>

      <h3>3.3 Changing Expectations</h3>

      <p>The internet environment in 2023 was different from the environment in which Omegle launched in 2009. Users, regulators and technology companies had become more aware of privacy, child safety, online abuse and platform responsibility.</p>

      <h2>So, Why Did Omegle Shut Down?</h2>

      <p>There was no single technical “shutdown button” or one law that explains the entire decision. The available evidence points to several interconnected factors.</p>

