      <h2>6. What Can Other Random-Chat Platforms Learn From Omegle?</h2>

      <p>Omegle's history provides useful lessons for any service that allows strangers to communicate online.</p>

      <h3>6.1 Safety Needs to Be Designed Into the Product</h3>

      <p>Safety should not depend entirely on users reporting problems after they occur. Platforms can combine community guidelines, reporting, blocking, automated detection and other controls to create multiple layers of protection.</p>

      <h3>6.2 Anonymity Requires Stronger User Controls</h3>

      <p>If users do not know who they are talking to, they need simple ways to leave conversations, report violations and protect their personal information.</p>

      <h3>6.3 Privacy Claims Should Be Specific</h3>

      <p>Platforms should clearly explain what information they collect, why it is collected and how long it is retained. Vague statements such as “completely private” do not tell users enough.</p>

      <h3>6.4 Safety and Sustainability Are Connected</h3>

      <p>A platform needs enough technical, financial and operational resources to maintain its safety systems over time. Building a feature once is not the same as operating a safe service at scale.</p>

      <h2>7. Omegle vs. Modern Random-Chat Services</h2>

      <p>Instead of claiming that one platform is automatically safer than another, users should compare the features and policies that actually affect their experience.</p>

